{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 conflates implementation diversity with true independence, yet the problem asks for evidence that distinguishes genuine independence from merely-uncorrelated-until-shared-cause failures. By conditioning its pro-decentralization claim on 'provided the failures are truly independent' without any evidentiary mechanism for establishing that condition, it presupposes exactly what the problem requires be identified. Its two counterconditions only surface shared-library/protocol bugs, but the problem's deeper challenge is that decentralized nodes may share latent environmental triggers (cloud-provider outage, TLS library, time-source) that look uncorrelated until the shared cause appears; SRC_001 gives no test for that. The mechanism's appeal to 'variety of implementations' is precisely the kind of surface diversity the problem warns may be illusory.","counterexample":["Two small services built by different teams, using different languages and consensus implementations, that independently chose the same cloud provider, DNS dependency, or TLS library version; no logic bug propagates between them, yet a provider outage or library bug causes simultaneous failure that was unobservable until the shared trigger occurred."],"target":"496395d4c8dde2aaa1e95222bbd28cc159ca2efaf8516161cce18f37df325a80"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's 'Redundancy Paradox' argument substitutes an empirical claim for the comparative reasoning the problem requires. It asserts that beyond a certain scale the coordination layer becomes a single point of failure (SPOF), but it never establishes the covering principle: why the same complexity-driven coordination failure cannot arise inside a decentralized network that itself needs coordination/protocol layers (service discovery, routing, consensus on shared state). A decentralized mesh using a shared protocol for interoperability can contain the same coordination-class SPOF it attributes only to the monolith. By treating 'absence of a global coordination failure' as a property of decentralization rather than a contingent design feature, it begs the question against the monolith and ignores the problem's demand to identify what evidence would distinguish true independence from apparent uncorrelation. It gives no distinguishing evidence; it only posits a structural disadvantage.","counterexample":["A decentralized network of small services that all depend on a shared service-mesh control plane or a shared certificate authority for mutual TLS; the coordination/identity layer is a global SPOF shared across all 'independent' nodes, so the claimed absence of global coordination failure is illusory."],"target":"5706c9666ec74b13c87130f0815c3e45953f719be12146a1c27c49eb69137350"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_004 proposes the 'Latent Trigger' test: check whether failure correlation spikes when an environmental variable changes, versus staying constant under true independence. But this is only sufficient under one evidentiary regime — discrete, observable environmental shifts. The problem asks to distinguish failures that are truly independent from failures that merely appear uncorrelated until a shared cause is found. SRC_04's own counterconditions concede the gap: a slow-burn shared degradation (e.g., a monotonic clock drift, gradual certificate expiry, creeping disk wear, slowly corrupting shared dataset) need not align with any discrete event, so its temporal-clustering test would report constant low correlation and falsely classify the failure as independent. The test therefore lacks the covering principle needed for the general case; it detects a subset of latent triggers (event-aligned) while remaining silent on the rest. Without a complementary mechanism (e.g., counterfactual/shared-dependency graph analysis, prospective stress tests of hypothesized common causes) it does not provide the evidence the problem demands.","counterexample":["Several small services fail at staggered times over weeks due to the same slowly-rotting shared CA certificate or gradually accumulating leap-second offset; there is no single environmental change point, temporal correlation remains low across the observation window, and SRC_04's test would declare the failures independent when they share a cause."],"target":"e7dfbbdd8ee3c6a26660767e69fa304f6aa79dc36f575e6319b60b4a43e1cb5c"}]}